  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Vertically stacked low pressure will meander \noff the NC and Mid-Atlantic coast through mid week bringing \nprolonged strong north to northeasterly winds across ENC. \nGuidance continues to show the low wobbling a bit off the coast \nwhile deepening tonight as it slowly lifts northward off the \nMid-Atlantic coast, as the system remains cut-off from the mean \nflow aloft. Winds may increase again tonight, with another surge\npotentially on Wednesday, so will make very little change to \nwinds at this time. It is not until Thursday that the low will \nbegin to accelerate away from the East Coast as a northern \nstream shortwave digs into the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast. \n\nTight pressure gradients between the offshore low and high \npressure across the Eastern CONUS will bring moderate north to \nnortheast winds across the region with the strongest winds \nacross the Outer Banks where gusts 35-40 mph will be possible. \nThe one change that was made this evening was to cancel the Wind\nAdvisory for the OBX. Winds haven't been as strong this \nevening, and guidance suggests winds should stay just below Wind\nAdvisory criteria through Wednesday. \n\nThe strong winds offshore continue to bring large surf along \nthe coast as well as coastal flooding impacts along vulnerable \nlocations of the coast, especially along Hatteras and Ocracoke \nIslands where dune structure is fragile. The National Parks \nService and NCDOT have recently reported substantial beach \nerosion from south Nags Head to Buxton, along with significant \noverwash occurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due to hazardous\nroad conditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed from the \nMarc Basnight Bridge to Rodanthe, and ferry service remains \nsuspended in certain locations. Additionally, webcams this \nevening show at least one section of the Pea Island dune \nstructure has been washed away.\n\nEach subsequent high tide cycle will continue to threaten the \ndunes further, and the threat of additional impacts to Hwy 12 \nremain. Minor soundside flooding is also a concern along \nsouthern portions of the Pamlico Sound, including Frisco and \nOcracoke. A Coastal Flood Warning remains in effect for Hatteras\nand Ocracoke Islands and a Coastal Flood Advisory remains in \neffect for the Northern Outer Banks. Additionally, a high surf \nadvisory continues for Cape Lookout and points north through the\nOBX. For Downeast Carteret County, the Coastal Flood Advisory \nhas been cancelled as water levels have receded, and are not \nanticipated to rise again.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass that has advected in\nwill remain with us through the next several days with temps a \nbit below climo. Maximum temperatures through the period are \nexpected to remain in the 60-70 degree range. Overnight lows \nwill range from the 40s inland to the 50s coast.",
